Instead of one helper, you create two helper Command Jobs. They don't even need to be inside the Top Box; they can sit outside of it acting as remote controls.
​1. The "Pause" Helper Job (JOB_PAUSE_TOPBOX)
This job executes a script that finds all jobs inside your Top Box, looks for the ones in ACTIVATED (AC) or INACTIVE (IN) states, and puts them ON_HOLD.
(Note: It is much safer to put them ON_HOLD rather than INACTIVE. If you force a time-dependent job back to INACTIVE, you risk stripping its time-check for that day. ON_HOLD safely freezes it where it is).
​The script inside this job would look something like this:

#!/bin/bash
TOP_BOX="YOUR_TOP_BOX_NAME"

# Find jobs in the box that are Activated (AC) or Inactive (IN)
JOBS_TO_HOLD=$(autorep -j $TOP_BOX -d | awk '$6 == "AC" || $6 == "IN" {print $1}')

# Loop through and put them on hold
for job in $JOBS_TO_HOLD; do
    sendevent -E ON_HOLD -J $job
done

# Finally, put the Top Box on hold
sendevent -E ON_HOLD -J $TOP_BOX


2. The "Resume" Helper Job (JOB_RESUME_TOPBOX)
This job simply reverses the process.

#!/bin/bash
TOP_BOX="YOUR_TOP_BOX_NAME"

# Find jobs in the box that are On Hold (OH)
JOBS_TO_RESUME=$(autorep -j $TOP_BOX -d | awk '$6 == "OH" {print $1}')

# Take the Top Box off hold first
sendevent -E OFF_HOLD -J $TOP_BOX

# Loop through and take children off hold
for job in $JOBS_TO_RESUME; do
    sendevent -E OFF_HOLD -J $job
done

